<attribute name="mainDescription" nodeId="c85f8035">&lt;p&gt;
    General estimating methods are categorized under two basic techniques – bottom-up and top-down. Both techniques are
    explained below and categorize tools and techniques associated with each (not an all-inclusive list).
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;
    Top-down estimation
&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Top-down estimation begins at the goal level and success defined. Methods under this technique include the following.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        &lt;strong&gt;Comparative (or analogous)&lt;/strong&gt; – estimates are based on extrapolating from one kind of activity
        estimate to another similar kind of activity; the assumption is that the work effort is comparable or analogous.
        Per PMBOK, this estimation is generally less costly and less time consuming, but also less accurate
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        &lt;strong&gt;Output-based heuristics&lt;/strong&gt; – estimates are based on rule-of-thumb information
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        &lt;strong&gt;Base and contingency&lt;/strong&gt; – estimates have two components - a base and a contingency, where the base is
        the minimum expected time required and the contingency is a percentage of the base to factor in a risk(s).
        Separating the base and contingency simplifies the estimation process
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;
    Bottom-up estimation
&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Bottom-up estimation begins with brainstorming to identify and subsequently categorize activities. The work effort is
    estimated for small activities and tasks and the numbers are aggregated. Methods under this technique include the
    following.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        &lt;strong&gt;Expert&lt;/strong&gt; – estimates are based on proven experience (e.g., subject matter resource)
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        &lt;strong&gt;Three-point (or PERT)&lt;/strong&gt; – estimates are based on a weighted average formula [tE = (tO + 4tM + tP)/6]
        using Most likely (tM), Optimistic (tO) and Pessimistic (tP) values to calculate the Expected (tE) activity
        duration. Most likely represents realistic expectations for an activity, Optimistic represents the best-case
        scenario for the activity and Pessimistic represents the worst-case scenario for the activity. The advantage to
        using the calculation is that it encapsulates risk factors in the estimation process
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        &lt;strong&gt;Formal estimating metrics&lt;/strong&gt; – estimates are based on historical data that is used to develop
        statistical estimating models using specific estimating elements and metrics&lt;br /&gt;
        &lt;br /&gt;
        Since projects are unique, there is no right approach to use. However, it is recommended that top-down and
        bottom-up estimation techniques are used in tandem to yield a more accurate result. Plus, using multiple approaches
        is a good way to cross-check an initial estimate. When developing project estimates, the team should account for
        project management effort and provide an auditable and repeatable estimation process with documented assumptions.
        The below graphic summarizes the estimation tools and techniques and provides examples of each.
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    &lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; src=&quot;./../../../core.mgmt.common.base/guidances/guidelines/resources/PM-600-0015.png&quot; width=&quot;600&quot; height=&quot;392&quot; /&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    A project cost can be associated by assigning individuals to the tasks, aggregating the hours for each individual and
    multiplying by that individual’s rate. Once this is done for each individual assigned to the project, the totals can be
    added together for total project cost.&lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
